<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhuge Liang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">181–234</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Kongming</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zhuge Liang was the chancellor of Shu Han in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms period</a> and chief adviser to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a>, whose alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-quan">Sun Quan</a> led to victory at Red Cliffs in 208. After Liu Bei's death he governed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-shan">Liu Shan</a>, pacified the south and led five northern expeditions against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a>. He became the model of the wise minister in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romance-of-the-three-kingdoms">Romance of the Three Kingdoms</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhuge-liang.jpg" alt="Zhuge Liang" width="320" height="649" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%98%8E%E4%BA%BA%E7%BB%98_%E3%80%8A%E8%AF%B8%E8%91%9B%E4%BA%AE%E5%83%8F%E3%80%8B%EF%BC%88%E5%8D%97%E8%96%B0%E6%AE%BF%E6%9C%AC%EF%BC%89.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
